Sorting in a **Singly Linked List (SLL)** is usually done with **Bubble Sort logic** because it’s easy to implement — even though it’s not the fastest.  

Here’s the **easiest way (swap data, not nodes)**:  

---

### 🔑 Core Code: Bubble Sort in SLL
```c
void sortList(struct Node* head) {
    if (head == NULL) return;  // Empty list

    struct Node* i, *j;
    int temp;

    for (i = head; i->next != NULL; i = i->next) {
        for (j = i->next; j != NULL; j = j->next) {
            if (i->data > j->data) {
                // Swap the data
                temp = i->data;
                i->data = j->data;
                j->data = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

---

### ⚡ How it works (Bubble Sort idea):
- Take one node (`i`), compare it with all nodes after it (`j`).  
- If `i->data > j->data` → swap their **data**.  
- Continue until all nodes are in ascending order.  

---

### Example
List before sorting:
```
head → [40] → [10] → [30] → [20] → NULL
```

Steps:  
- Compare 40 & 10 → swap → `[10] → [40] → [30] → [20]`  
- Compare 40 & 30 → swap → `[10] → [30] → [40] → [20]`  
- Compare 40 & 20 → swap → `[10] → [30] → [20] → [40]`  
- Keep doing until fully sorted.

Final sorted list:
```
head → [10] → [20] → [30] → [40] → NULL
```

---

